1
00:00:00,417 --> 00:00:01,750
<i>Previously on "The Ark"...</i>

2
00:00:03,044 --> 00:00:06,171
Lieutenant Brice?
It's me, Dr. Morozov.

3
00:00:06,172 --> 00:00:07,631
You really think
they're down there?

4
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:09,133
Only one way to find out.

5
00:00:09,134 --> 00:00:10,551
We have to get a signal
to "Ark 1."

6
00:00:10,552 --> 00:00:13,345
Slide under the door first.

7
00:00:13,346 --> 00:00:14,930
I've got to read
all about you.

8
00:00:14,931 --> 00:00:17,766
I really wished
you'd let this go.

9
00:00:18,893 --> 00:00:20,060
Yes! It sent.

10
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:24,064
You have to fix me.

11
00:00:27,819 --> 00:00:30,029
What the hell?

12
00:00:37,078 --> 00:00:39,955
So the message arrived with
a looping distress signal.

13
00:00:39,956 --> 00:00:41,915
A home beacon--
it's coming from Ross.

14
00:00:41,916 --> 00:00:44,043
Can you pinpoint
its location on the planet?

15
00:00:44,044 --> 00:00:45,712
We'd have to be
much closer for that.

16
00:00:48,256 --> 00:00:50,466
I want to believe this
as much as you do, Ava,

17
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:53,552
but I saw Brice die
with my own eyes.

18
00:00:53,553 --> 00:00:56,096
I don't see how
it could be him.

19
00:00:56,097 --> 00:00:58,557
The signal came on
a secure GSA frequency

20
00:00:58,558 --> 00:01:01,101
embedded with
Brice's ID number.

21
00:01:01,102 --> 00:01:02,936
It would take
someone with access

22
00:01:02,937 --> 00:01:06,190
to proprietary GSA passwords
and information to fake it.

23
00:01:06,191 --> 00:01:07,941
So even if it's not James--

24
00:01:07,942 --> 00:01:10,028
Someone is asking for help.

25
00:01:12,238 --> 00:01:14,990
We leave in an hour.
- I'm coming with you.

26
00:01:14,991 --> 00:01:16,992
Damn straight you are.

27
00:01:27,754 --> 00:01:30,255
You're giving him blood?

28
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:33,300
His blood pressure
dropped last night.

29
00:01:33,301 --> 00:01:35,094
He has some internal bleeding.

30
00:01:35,095 --> 00:01:37,679
I'm giving him clotting agents.

31
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:40,182
Are you going somewhere?

32
00:01:40,183 --> 00:01:41,642
"Ark 1" needs me,

33
00:01:41,643 --> 00:01:44,937
and, to be honest,
I need some space.

34
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:46,730
Can we talk about it?

35
00:01:46,731 --> 00:01:49,233
Um, not right now.

36
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:51,444
"Ark 1" leaves in 45 minutes.

37
00:01:53,697 --> 00:01:56,198
Okay.

38
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:59,493
Look, the time away,
it'll do us some good.

39
00:01:59,494 --> 00:02:02,955
I can clear my head up there.

40
00:02:02,956 --> 00:02:05,083
I understand.

41
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:09,045
We're both just
doing our best.

42
00:02:11,089 --> 00:02:13,633
Promise me you'll be
careful out there, okay?

43
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:17,636
Yeah.

44
00:02:17,637 --> 00:02:18,722
Hey.

45
00:02:21,349 --> 00:02:23,393
Let's talk when I get back.

46
00:02:26,646 --> 00:02:28,481
I'll be here.

47
00:02:30,859 --> 00:02:33,068
Admiral Hammond knows Ross
better than we do,

48
00:02:33,069 --> 00:02:34,111
but I can't raise him on comms,

49
00:02:34,112 --> 00:02:35,696
and we don't have time
to wait for him.

50
00:02:35,697 --> 00:02:37,614
If I find him before
you're out of range,

51
00:02:37,615 --> 00:02:39,324
I'll put him on
with you for guidance.

52
00:02:39,325 --> 00:02:41,368
Thank you.

53
00:02:41,369 --> 00:02:43,329
Are you sure
you don't want me to come?

54
00:02:43,330 --> 00:02:45,122
Stay with Angus.
Enjoy your time together.

55
00:02:45,123 --> 00:02:46,665
Thanks.

56
00:02:46,666 --> 00:02:48,334
And the adjustments
I made to the shields

57
00:02:48,335 --> 00:02:49,626
will give you more time
in Ross's solar storms.

58
00:02:49,627 --> 00:02:51,295
Just remember to reroute
no more than 10%

59
00:02:51,296 --> 00:02:52,713
of the gravitons
to "Ark 1's" exterior.

60
00:02:52,714 --> 00:02:56,467
For more power, you can--
- Alicia, don't worry.

61
00:02:56,468 --> 00:02:59,011
I've got it.
- Okay.

62
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:01,930
Good luck.
I hope you find Brice.

63
00:03:01,931 --> 00:03:04,099
Thanks.

64
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:12,710
Dr. Marsh.

65
00:04:17,257 --> 00:04:18,340
Clear.

66
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:23,053
Clear.

67
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:32,438
Clear.

68
00:04:36,568 --> 00:04:39,862
Time of death, 10:45.

69
00:04:46,077 --> 00:04:47,828
So once Kelly and I
got out of the chamber,

70
00:04:47,829 --> 00:04:50,622
there were, like,
10 of them waiting for us.

71
00:04:50,623 --> 00:04:52,624
Then Alicia scared
them off with her WD.

72
00:04:52,625 --> 00:04:54,251
We all feared the worst.

73
00:04:54,252 --> 00:04:55,586
How well did you see them?

74
00:04:55,587 --> 00:04:57,254
Well enough to see
they were human.

75
00:04:57,255 --> 00:04:58,505
They're not human.

76
00:04:58,506 --> 00:04:59,923
They definitely
looked like it.

77
00:04:59,924 --> 00:05:01,216
Like our John Doe?

78
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:03,052
I think he was one of them.

79
00:05:03,053 --> 00:05:04,928
He was wearing Angus's clothes.

80
00:05:04,929 --> 00:05:07,306
Unless all ten of them
stowed away on one

81
00:05:07,307 --> 00:05:08,932
or more of our ships, got here,

82
00:05:08,933 --> 00:05:11,185
and then somehow built
a giant underground complex

83
00:05:11,186 --> 00:05:13,103
in a few weeks,
they're not human,

84
00:05:13,104 --> 00:05:15,147
and to think so is idiotic.

85
00:05:15,148 --> 00:05:17,483
What Mr. Trust
is trying to say,

86
00:05:17,484 --> 00:05:19,860
in his usual sweet way,

87
00:05:19,861 --> 00:05:23,572
is that evolving here
to be identical to us--

88
00:05:23,573 --> 00:05:25,324
Except their ears
and vocal cords.

89
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:27,534
Yes, even with
those differences,

90
00:05:27,535 --> 00:05:30,204
the odds of that sort
of parallel evolution

91
00:05:30,205 --> 00:05:32,873
are astronomical.
- But not zero.

92
00:05:32,874 --> 00:05:34,917
And this isn't the first thing
we've seen on Homebase

93
00:05:34,918 --> 00:05:37,086
that kind of seems like it.
- Such as?

94
00:05:37,087 --> 00:05:38,420
The trees.

95
00:05:38,421 --> 00:05:40,631
They look like Earth trees.

96
00:05:40,632 --> 00:05:42,800
And, I mean, I haven't had
a chance to test the dead bird

97
00:05:42,801 --> 00:05:45,135
we found yet since
we got snatched and all,

98
00:05:45,136 --> 00:05:47,846
but it sure looked like
a Eurasian blackbird.

99
00:05:47,847 --> 00:05:49,848
So test it.
Test everything.

100
00:05:49,849 --> 00:05:51,266
I think you'll find
it's as much about Earth

101
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:53,227
as those cave creatures.

102
00:05:53,228 --> 00:05:57,481
In the meantime, whoever--
whatever these people are,

103
00:05:57,482 --> 00:05:59,608
they're obviously intelligent.

104
00:05:59,609 --> 00:06:00,818
We should try and
communicate with them,

105
00:06:00,819 --> 00:06:03,237
see if we can get some answers.
- I don't agree.

106
00:06:03,238 --> 00:06:06,532
We don't know what they had
planned for Angus and Kelly,

107
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:08,951
and one of them already
put our sheriff

108
00:06:08,952 --> 00:06:11,203
in critical condition
in Med Bay.

109
00:06:11,204 --> 00:06:15,207
Has anyone studied
the garments

110
00:06:15,208 --> 00:06:16,542
they had Angus dressed in?

111
00:06:16,543 --> 00:06:18,252
They appear to be
made from a fabric

112
00:06:18,253 --> 00:06:19,878
derived from local vegetation.

113
00:06:19,879 --> 00:06:21,422
Which might be
Earth vegetation.

114
00:06:21,423 --> 00:06:22,840
Or might not.

115
00:06:22,841 --> 00:06:25,050
Listen, just let me
go back out there

116
00:06:25,051 --> 00:06:27,511
and collect fresh plant
and tree samples,

117
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:29,972
perform a full analysis
DNA spectroscopy.

118
00:06:29,973 --> 00:06:31,598
You want to go back
to the forest

119
00:06:31,599 --> 00:06:32,975
where we just rescued
you and Kelly from?

120
00:06:32,976 --> 00:06:34,935
We can send
an armed security officer

121
00:06:34,936 --> 00:06:37,062
to accompany you.
- I vote against it.

122
00:06:37,063 --> 00:06:38,898
It's too risky.
Avega?

123
00:06:41,109 --> 00:06:45,446
I think we should
let him look further,

124
00:06:45,447 --> 00:06:48,240
but limited to daylight hours.

125
00:06:48,241 --> 00:06:51,285
I guess I'm outvoted.

126
00:06:51,286 --> 00:06:55,748
If you insist on going back,
please be careful out there.

127
00:06:55,749 --> 00:06:57,374
I'm going too.

128
00:06:57,375 --> 00:06:59,251
I'm not letting Angus
out of my sight.

129
00:07:00,587 --> 00:07:03,338
<i>Ms. Ilic,
please report to Med Bay.</i>

130
00:07:03,339 --> 00:07:04,549
Excuse me.

131
00:07:06,551 --> 00:07:08,844
Oh, Mr. Trust,
we could really

132
00:07:08,845 --> 00:07:10,471
use your knowledge out there.

133
00:07:10,472 --> 00:07:13,474
I think not.
If you'd excuse me.

134
00:07:13,475 --> 00:07:16,769
I hear lunch
is very good today.

135
00:07:23,651 --> 00:07:27,821
Last I heard, he was stable.

136
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:30,699
He went into cardiac arrest.

137
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:32,034
I tried to save him,
but I couldn't.

138
00:07:32,035 --> 00:07:34,620
I'm sorry.
- I don't trust you, Marsh.

139
00:07:34,621 --> 00:07:36,997
First Kabir tells me
Milan was abusive.

140
00:07:36,998 --> 00:07:40,042
Next thing I know,
he dies under your care.

141
00:07:40,043 --> 00:07:41,752
No doctor wants
to lose a patient,

142
00:07:41,753 --> 00:07:44,171
no matter who they are.
I know he meant a lot to you.

143
00:07:44,172 --> 00:07:45,632
I'm very sorry.

144
00:07:47,258 --> 00:07:49,301
This isn't over.

145
00:08:04,567 --> 00:08:07,486
Hey, you see those stolons?

146
00:08:07,487 --> 00:08:09,196
The narrow blades
folded at the midrib?

147
00:08:09,197 --> 00:08:11,198
Pointed tips like a monocot.

148
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:12,825
We might have something here.

149
00:08:12,826 --> 00:08:14,284
What's it look like?

150
00:08:14,285 --> 00:08:17,287
Poaceae maybe?

151
00:08:17,288 --> 00:08:21,166
Can you imagine if it was
a match for a species on Earth?

152
00:08:21,167 --> 00:08:23,627
Like--like,
what would that even mean?

153
00:08:26,423 --> 00:08:28,132
What?

154
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:29,883
I'm being serious.

155
00:08:29,884 --> 00:08:32,720
I just missed you,
that's all.

156
00:08:32,721 --> 00:08:34,680
Ah.
Thank you.

157
00:08:42,313 --> 00:08:45,065
Alicia?

158
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:54,491
Is that Admiral Hammond?

159
00:08:54,492 --> 00:08:57,120
Okay.

160
00:09:04,002 --> 00:09:05,502
Preparing to disengage FTL.

161
00:09:05,503 --> 00:09:06,879
Stand by, Eva.

162
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:10,090
In three, two, one.

163
00:09:11,843 --> 00:09:14,094
Shields activated.

164
00:09:14,095 --> 00:09:15,763
Here we are again.

165
00:09:18,308 --> 00:09:21,769
- Eva?
- Stand by.

166
00:09:21,770 --> 00:09:22,811
Eva?

167
00:09:30,570 --> 00:09:31,945
We're good now.

168
00:09:31,946 --> 00:09:34,491
Shields will hold
for ten hours.

169
00:09:35,867 --> 00:09:37,576
Okay.

170
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:40,746
10 hours of shields,
and, uh, only 92 minutes

171
00:09:40,747 --> 00:09:42,039
until the sun sets
on the Ross colony.

172
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:45,042
We need to move fast.
- EVA suits?

173
00:09:45,043 --> 00:09:46,085
Didn't need them last time,

174
00:09:46,086 --> 00:09:47,753
and it's hot as hell
down there.

175
00:09:47,754 --> 00:09:49,171
All right, no EVA suits.

176
00:09:49,172 --> 00:09:51,132
Dr. Kabir, meet us
in the shuttle dock.

177
00:10:01,559 --> 00:10:03,060
Make sure your straps
are tight.

178
00:10:03,061 --> 00:10:04,687
This will get bumpy.

179
00:10:07,732 --> 00:10:09,775
Once we're through,
let's track the distress signal

180
00:10:09,776 --> 00:10:11,819
and touch down near its source.

181
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:15,864
- You mean Brice.
- Uh, hopefully.

182
00:10:15,865 --> 00:10:18,535
We need to get him and get back
in the shuttle before sunset.

183
00:10:31,589 --> 00:10:33,882
Looks like it might be
coming from the colony.

184
00:10:33,883 --> 00:10:36,260
Heading in that direction.

185
00:10:36,261 --> 00:10:38,095
Signal's getting stronger.

186
00:10:42,851 --> 00:10:45,519
Looks like someone
dropped a bomb on it.

187
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:47,730
This is where you were
attacked by those creatures?

188
00:10:47,731 --> 00:10:48,939
Yeah.

189
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:51,025
They were attacking
the building when we left.

190
00:10:51,026 --> 00:10:54,445
I guess they finished the job.

191
00:10:54,446 --> 00:10:56,030
No one could survive
in there.

192
00:10:56,031 --> 00:11:00,409
Yeah, which means no one
can send a signal from there.

193
00:11:00,410 --> 00:11:04,496
Look, we're honing in
on the signal.

194
00:11:04,497 --> 00:11:06,665
Oh, yeah, that's far away
from the structures.

195
00:11:06,666 --> 00:11:07,916
What is that?

196
00:11:07,917 --> 00:11:09,626
The noise from
the solar flare.

197
00:11:09,627 --> 00:11:12,338
Do you mind, Captain?
- Please.

198
00:11:17,135 --> 00:11:19,803
There.
- Should I adjust course?

199
00:11:19,804 --> 00:11:22,139
15 degrees right,
6 kilometers out.

200
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:24,183
Copy.

201
00:11:29,939 --> 00:11:33,400
Looks like it's somewhere
inside that forest.

202
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:35,027
I don't see any structures.

203
00:11:35,028 --> 00:11:37,071
There's no indication of any.

204
00:11:37,072 --> 00:11:39,490
Beacon is 2.1 kilometers in.

205
00:11:43,662 --> 00:11:45,079
Okay.

206
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:47,206
Close enough if we hurry.

207
00:11:47,207 --> 00:11:49,500
Set down there
by the tree line.

208
00:11:49,501 --> 00:11:51,668
We'll hoof it from there.

209
00:11:58,009 --> 00:12:00,302
Well, too bad Angus
isn't here.

210
00:12:00,303 --> 00:12:02,262
These trees are the same
as the ones on Homebase.

211
00:12:02,263 --> 00:12:04,473
Which makes no sense.

212
00:12:04,474 --> 00:12:05,933
Do we need to be worried
about those creatures

213
00:12:05,934 --> 00:12:08,018
coming out for a snack?

214
00:12:08,019 --> 00:12:10,521
I'm not reading
any heat signatures.

215
00:12:10,522 --> 00:12:15,151
I think we're clear.
- Okay. Everyone ready?

216
00:12:17,362 --> 00:12:20,114
Eva?
- Are you okay, Eva?

217
00:12:20,115 --> 00:12:21,365
Yeah.
Oh.

218
00:12:21,366 --> 00:12:24,576
Uh, I guess
I'm a little nervous.

219
00:12:24,577 --> 00:12:27,538
The tracker startled me.
- Understandable.

220
00:12:27,539 --> 00:12:31,417
Hey, no matter what,
we're here for you, okay?

221
00:12:31,418 --> 00:12:34,086
Yeah.
Thank you.

222
00:12:34,087 --> 00:12:36,213
All right, we have 26 minutes
until we have to turn back.

223
00:12:36,214 --> 00:12:38,215
Let's be smart with our time.

224
00:12:54,774 --> 00:12:56,400
Is that--

225
00:12:56,401 --> 00:12:58,569
Completely identical
to the one on Homebase?

226
00:12:58,570 --> 00:13:00,529
Yeah, does anyone
have a good explanation

227
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:02,364
for two identical structures
on planets

228
00:13:02,365 --> 00:13:05,284
32 light years apart?
- That's not all.

229
00:13:14,669 --> 00:13:17,838
On Homebase, Angus's WD could
barely get through the walls.

230
00:13:17,839 --> 00:13:20,174
How did we receive
Brice's signal from here?

231
00:13:20,175 --> 00:13:21,759
It seems like a trap.

232
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:24,011
Why else would the door
just be propped open?

233
00:13:24,012 --> 00:13:25,637
A trap by who?

234
00:13:25,638 --> 00:13:28,265
Well, maybe this planet has
its own people living in caves?

235
00:13:28,266 --> 00:13:29,600
We don't want
to end up locked

236
00:13:29,601 --> 00:13:30,893
in there like Angus and Kelly.

237
00:13:30,894 --> 00:13:32,853
Well, we can't just
leave James in there.

238
00:13:32,854 --> 00:13:34,313
If he's in there.

239
00:13:34,314 --> 00:13:35,522
Look, I'm going in
with or without you--

240
00:13:35,523 --> 00:13:37,983
Hold on!
Hold on.

241
00:13:37,984 --> 00:13:40,819
No one's going rogue.

242
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:41,905
Kimi, what do you think?

243
00:13:45,784 --> 00:13:48,619
We're armed.
I say we go in.

244
00:13:48,620 --> 00:13:50,662
I agree.
Eva and I will take lead.

245
00:13:50,663 --> 00:13:53,540
Kimi, watch our six.
Ian, cover our flanks.

246
00:13:53,541 --> 00:13:55,626
Sanji, stay by me.
- Mm-hmm.

247
00:13:55,627 --> 00:13:56,668
Got it.

248
00:14:29,327 --> 00:14:32,413
It looks just like the inside
of the Homebase tunnel.

249
00:14:32,414 --> 00:14:33,998
But how?

250
00:14:33,999 --> 00:14:36,959
Better question is,
who built it?

251
00:14:47,929 --> 00:14:49,639
This way.

252
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:55,978
We're getting close.

253
00:15:13,872 --> 00:15:14,831
Oh.

254
00:15:16,583 --> 00:15:19,126
It's coming from
the other side of this wall.

255
00:15:19,127 --> 00:15:20,294
Okay, everyone, spread out.

256
00:15:20,295 --> 00:15:22,212
See if you can find
some way in,

257
00:15:22,213 --> 00:15:25,591
uh, some kind of door
or opening--

258
00:15:26,926 --> 00:15:29,011
Everyone, back!

259
00:15:43,068 --> 00:15:46,236
Kimi, stand guard.

260
00:16:05,006 --> 00:16:07,174
Oh, God.

261
00:16:07,175 --> 00:16:09,635
Is he even alive?

262
00:16:09,636 --> 00:16:12,429
Please say he's alive.

263
00:16:15,350 --> 00:16:16,558
Get him free, now!

264
00:16:23,775 --> 00:16:25,442
Eva, come on!
Eva.

265
00:16:25,443 --> 00:16:26,736
Wait.

266
00:16:28,947 --> 00:16:30,573
Get this.

267
00:16:32,951 --> 00:16:34,077
You got this.

268
00:16:41,751 --> 00:16:43,837
Easy.
Easy.

269
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:48,049
It's okay.

270
00:17:21,082 --> 00:17:22,791
Dr. Marsh's
medical report ruled

271
00:17:22,792 --> 00:17:25,169
that Hammond died
of a stab wound

272
00:17:25,170 --> 00:17:28,005
caused by a large blade.

273
00:17:28,006 --> 00:17:30,883
Marsh,
is he the best we can do

274
00:17:30,884 --> 00:17:32,134
for a medical examiner?

275
00:17:32,135 --> 00:17:34,428
I am very, very sorry
about Sheriff Petrovic--

276
00:17:34,429 --> 00:17:36,847
I am--
but Marsh is a good doctor.

277
00:17:36,848 --> 00:17:40,351
- Agree to disagree.
- Hm.

278
00:17:40,352 --> 00:17:43,062
Any other noteworthy findings?

279
00:17:43,063 --> 00:17:45,856
The woods were
just a dump site.

280
00:17:45,857 --> 00:17:47,566
Hammond was murdered elsewhere

281
00:17:47,567 --> 00:17:48,984
and then dragged to where
he was discovered.

282
00:17:48,985 --> 00:17:50,861
Ah.

283
00:17:50,862 --> 00:17:54,156
Am I alone in suspecting
these people,

284
00:17:54,157 --> 00:17:57,910
or whatever they are,
in the caves?

285
00:17:57,911 --> 00:18:01,080
The thought
did cross my mind.

286
00:18:01,081 --> 00:18:05,334
Let's gather all the facts
before we draw any conclusions.

287
00:18:05,335 --> 00:18:07,044
There's no murder weapon yet,

288
00:18:07,045 --> 00:18:09,588
but a security detail
is combing the area now.

289
00:18:09,589 --> 00:18:12,926
We appreciate you taking
the lead on this, Felix.

290
00:18:24,145 --> 00:18:26,481
You all right, Dr. Marsh?

291
00:18:28,441 --> 00:18:30,985
It never gets easier
to lose a patient.

292
00:18:30,986 --> 00:18:33,278
Even that one?

293
00:18:33,279 --> 00:18:35,489
What's that supposed to mean?

294
00:18:35,490 --> 00:18:37,908
Well, I noticed you
and Dr. Kabir's relationship

295
00:18:37,909 --> 00:18:39,993
is sort of on the rocks.

296
00:18:39,994 --> 00:18:42,496
We have hit a rough spot,
yes,

297
00:18:42,497 --> 00:18:43,998
but what does that have to do
with that patient?

298
00:18:43,999 --> 00:18:45,250
I looked him up.

299
00:18:46,835 --> 00:18:49,169
Sheriff Petrovic was
Dr. Kabir's ex-husband.

300
00:18:49,170 --> 00:18:52,047
Did you know that?
- You looked him up?

301
00:18:52,048 --> 00:18:55,300
- I was curious.
- How is that your business?

302
00:18:55,301 --> 00:18:56,677
I suppose it's not.

303
00:18:56,678 --> 00:19:00,265
Is that what caused
this rough spot?

304
00:19:03,143 --> 00:19:05,102
Why do you have
so many questions, Jelena?

305
00:19:05,103 --> 00:19:07,187
I was just wondering
if Dr. Kabir's

306
00:19:07,188 --> 00:19:09,023
former relationship
had anything to do

307
00:19:09,024 --> 00:19:11,900
with you hesitating
when he coded.

308
00:19:11,901 --> 00:19:14,611
I didn't hesitate.

309
00:19:14,612 --> 00:19:16,280
I had to check
the readings to decide

310
00:19:16,281 --> 00:19:19,408
the proper course of action.

311
00:19:19,409 --> 00:19:20,618
Okay.

312
00:19:22,454 --> 00:19:23,746
Are you accusing me
of something?

313
00:19:23,747 --> 00:19:25,373
No, not at all.

314
00:19:28,668 --> 00:19:32,296
Jelena...

315
00:19:32,297 --> 00:19:33,714
I'm asking you as a friend,

316
00:19:33,715 --> 00:19:37,551
please use discretion
when discussing this

317
00:19:37,552 --> 00:19:39,553
with anyone else.

318
00:19:39,554 --> 00:19:41,972
Of course.

319
00:19:58,656 --> 00:20:01,575
Any luck pulling the data
from Hammond's WD?

320
00:20:01,576 --> 00:20:03,035
No.

321
00:20:03,036 --> 00:20:05,788
The killer must have wiped it,
along with the remote backups.

322
00:20:05,789 --> 00:20:08,207
Doesn't sound like
a person from the caves.

323
00:20:08,208 --> 00:20:11,043
I doubt they could use
our tech.

324
00:20:11,044 --> 00:20:13,504
Thank you for trying.
- You're welcome.

325
00:20:13,505 --> 00:20:16,632
But I was thinking,
and I realized,

326
00:20:16,633 --> 00:20:20,761
WDs aren't the only thing
that leave a digital footprint.

327
00:20:20,762 --> 00:20:23,055
I checked Hammond's
database activity.

328
00:20:23,056 --> 00:20:25,682
Without admin codes, I had to
bypass encryption algorithms.

329
00:20:25,683 --> 00:20:27,142
I thought it would
be way harder, but--

330
00:20:27,143 --> 00:20:30,187
- You found something.
- Sorry, yeah.

331
00:20:30,188 --> 00:20:31,689
Based on Admiral Hammond's
searches,

332
00:20:31,690 --> 00:20:34,233
it seemed like he was
super interested in Ves.

333
00:20:34,234 --> 00:20:36,235
Like, kind of obsessed.

334
00:20:36,236 --> 00:20:38,779
- Ves? Ves Ilic?
- Yeah.

335
00:20:38,780 --> 00:20:40,155
It seemed like
he was trying to find out

336
00:20:40,156 --> 00:20:43,283
everything he could about her.
Here.

337
00:20:43,284 --> 00:20:45,953
Take a look.

338
00:20:45,954 --> 00:20:48,956
What's that missing text?

339
00:20:48,957 --> 00:20:51,375
Class 3 GSA redactions.

340
00:20:51,376 --> 00:20:53,836
Why? Who is she?

341
00:20:53,837 --> 00:20:56,213
I'm not sure yet.

342
00:20:56,214 --> 00:21:00,718
In the meantime,
do not share this with anyone.

343
00:21:00,719 --> 00:21:02,470
Of course.

344
00:21:04,389 --> 00:21:06,223
We don't have
much time to waste

345
00:21:06,224 --> 00:21:08,226
before she covers her tracks.

346
00:21:14,816 --> 00:21:17,818
Well, try.

347
00:21:17,819 --> 00:21:22,573
Try to find someone willing
to share with either of them.

348
00:21:22,574 --> 00:21:25,909
Okay, bye. Thanks for calling.

349
00:21:25,910 --> 00:21:27,369
Um, everything all right?

350
00:21:27,370 --> 00:21:29,997
Marital spat.

351
00:21:29,998 --> 00:21:32,124
Wife wants husband to move out,

352
00:21:32,125 --> 00:21:35,753
but we don't have anywhere else
for him to move in.

353
00:21:35,754 --> 00:21:38,172
How can I help you?

354
00:21:38,173 --> 00:21:40,924
Yes, it's come
to my attention

355
00:21:40,925 --> 00:21:45,137
that Admiral Hammond had taken
quite an interest in you.

356
00:21:45,138 --> 00:21:49,224
Really?
You're joking.

357
00:21:49,225 --> 00:21:51,269
Any idea why?

358
00:21:52,687 --> 00:21:55,481
Well, he seemed to be
attracted to me.

359
00:21:55,482 --> 00:21:57,525
It was kind of flattering.

360
00:21:59,944 --> 00:22:03,947
I think it's more than that.

361
00:22:03,948 --> 00:22:05,449
How so?

362
00:22:05,450 --> 00:22:07,701
He was looking
at your records.

363
00:22:07,702 --> 00:22:10,287
That's odd.

364
00:22:10,288 --> 00:22:13,082
He must have been
quite bored with them.

365
00:22:13,083 --> 00:22:14,416
Hmm.

366
00:22:14,417 --> 00:22:16,752
You didn't have
any previous relationship?

367
00:22:16,753 --> 00:22:21,256
No.
I just met the man.

368
00:22:21,257 --> 00:22:22,549
You were aboard
the first Ark that arrived

369
00:22:22,550 --> 00:22:24,718
here on Trappist, correct?
- Yes.

370
00:22:24,719 --> 00:22:27,221
And what does that have to do
with Admiral Hammond?

371
00:22:27,222 --> 00:22:30,099
I'm just doing
my due diligence.

372
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:32,810
Ah.

373
00:22:32,811 --> 00:22:34,561
Well, I appreciate that.

374
00:22:34,562 --> 00:22:41,443
I'm always available if
you have additional questions.

375
00:22:41,444 --> 00:22:44,947
Thank you for your time.

376
00:23:19,274 --> 00:23:20,316
We found something!

377
00:23:29,200 --> 00:23:32,119
Mr. Strickland,
is that your sword?

378
00:24:00,815 --> 00:24:02,691
Is someone there?

379
00:24:14,454 --> 00:24:16,872
But you don't seem
to have any wounds.

380
00:24:16,873 --> 00:24:18,416
I don't understand.

381
00:24:20,919 --> 00:24:22,544
I guess I was
pretty bad off, eh?

382
00:24:22,545 --> 00:24:24,088
You have no idea.

383
00:24:24,089 --> 00:24:27,341
That's impossible.
I--I saw you die.

384
00:24:27,342 --> 00:24:30,427
You were torn apart.
- It was Doc.

385
00:24:30,428 --> 00:24:33,097
She nursed me back to health.
- Doc Morozov?

386
00:24:33,098 --> 00:24:34,598
Aye.
Yeah, she saved my life.

387
00:24:34,599 --> 00:24:36,475
Not one stitch, one scar.

388
00:24:36,476 --> 00:24:38,435
Wait, how do we even know
this is Brice?

389
00:24:38,436 --> 00:24:40,021
What the hell kind
of question is that?

390
00:24:41,773 --> 00:24:42,981
What's your favorite scotch?

391
00:24:42,982 --> 00:24:44,316
I hate scotch.

392
00:24:44,317 --> 00:24:45,859
Yeah, it's him.

393
00:24:45,860 --> 00:24:48,696
James, do you know
how you got here?

394
00:24:48,697 --> 00:24:50,948
Uh, I don't--I don't know.

395
00:24:50,949 --> 00:24:52,074
Where am I?

396
00:24:52,075 --> 00:24:54,034
An underground cave on Ross.

397
00:24:54,035 --> 00:24:56,370
What's the last thing
you remember?

398
00:24:56,371 --> 00:25:01,542
I was in one of
the "Ark 4" structures.

399
00:25:01,543 --> 00:25:03,419
Everything went dark,
and then I just--

400
00:25:03,420 --> 00:25:04,461
I woke up here.

401
00:25:04,462 --> 00:25:05,713
You were in "Ark 4"
structures?

402
00:25:05,714 --> 00:25:07,131
Yeah, of course.

403
00:25:07,132 --> 00:25:08,549
Where else can
we send a signal?

404
00:25:08,550 --> 00:25:10,801
- We?
- Yes, me and Doc.

405
00:25:10,802 --> 00:25:14,264
We--she got injured.

406
00:25:15,807 --> 00:25:17,599
Listen, we need to go.
We need to go.

407
00:25:17,600 --> 00:25:20,395
We need to help her now.
- James, Morozov is dead.

408
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:26,150
Shit.
Shit.

409
00:25:26,151 --> 00:25:27,902
Oh, shit.

410
00:25:30,071 --> 00:25:32,323
Was it the Raiders?
- Raiders?

411
00:25:32,324 --> 00:25:33,782
Aye, the Raiders.
They--they came in.

412
00:25:33,783 --> 00:25:35,784
They were heavily armed.
They shot her.

413
00:25:35,785 --> 00:25:38,287
There weren't any other ships
in orbit when we arrived.

414
00:25:38,288 --> 00:25:40,164
Look, I know what I saw, okay?

415
00:25:40,165 --> 00:25:41,665
We're wasting time.
We need to go.

416
00:25:41,666 --> 00:25:44,668
We need to go now, okay?
- James, Doc is dead.

417
00:25:44,669 --> 00:25:46,336
You and I both saw her die.

418
00:25:46,337 --> 00:25:48,047
I would remember that, okay?

419
00:25:48,048 --> 00:25:52,384
No, I am sorry,
James, but I do.

420
00:25:52,385 --> 00:25:54,928
I saw her die.

421
00:25:54,929 --> 00:25:56,930
Well, you said
you saw me die as well.

422
00:25:56,931 --> 00:25:58,891
And I'm right here, aren't I?
- Okay.

423
00:25:58,892 --> 00:26:01,894
All right, look,
where did you see her last?

424
00:26:01,895 --> 00:26:03,354
The "Ark 4" comms array.

425
00:26:30,131 --> 00:26:31,632
We're running out of time.

426
00:26:31,633 --> 00:26:33,717
Kimi, we might need
a hand in here.

427
00:26:36,638 --> 00:26:38,055
Kimi?

428
00:26:42,727 --> 00:26:44,479
Garnet to Kimi, do you copy?

429
00:26:46,064 --> 00:26:47,439
Ian, let's go find her.

430
00:26:47,440 --> 00:26:48,941
Eva, Dr. Kabir,
get him hydrated

431
00:26:48,942 --> 00:26:51,568
and ready to move out.
- Yeah.

432
00:26:58,326 --> 00:27:00,452
What would make her
wander off?

433
00:27:00,453 --> 00:27:02,414
She wouldn't.

434
00:27:20,557 --> 00:27:22,516
In the tunnels on Homebase,
did the walls open

435
00:27:22,517 --> 00:27:23,685
in the same way?

436
00:27:26,021 --> 00:27:27,564
Sharon?

437
00:27:30,692 --> 00:27:32,776
Sharon!

438
00:27:37,198 --> 00:27:39,116
Thanks for coming to get me.

439
00:27:39,117 --> 00:27:40,617
What was I supposed to do?

440
00:27:40,618 --> 00:27:43,288
You missed our call.

441
00:27:45,081 --> 00:27:47,791
- Sharon's gone!
- What?

442
00:27:47,792 --> 00:27:49,001
She disappeared.

443
00:27:49,002 --> 00:27:51,045
She was right beside me,
and she disappeared.

444
00:27:51,046 --> 00:27:53,048
- Let's go find her.
- Yeah.

445
00:27:55,300 --> 00:27:57,968
Sharon!

446
00:27:57,969 --> 00:27:59,887
Stay close, all right?

447
00:27:59,888 --> 00:28:01,764
Kimi?

448
00:28:01,765 --> 00:28:04,183
Don't take your eyes
off each other, all right,

449
00:28:04,184 --> 00:28:05,768
not even for a second.
That's all it takes.

450
00:28:05,769 --> 00:28:07,186
She couldn't have gone far.

451
00:28:07,187 --> 00:28:08,812
There are probably
other rooms

452
00:28:08,813 --> 00:28:10,064
like the one we found you in.

453
00:28:10,065 --> 00:28:11,982
Yeah, but the trouble
is finding them.

454
00:28:11,983 --> 00:28:14,902
Sharon!

455
00:28:17,405 --> 00:28:20,492
Sharon, is that you?
Sharon!

456
00:28:23,703 --> 00:28:26,705
Sharon!
- Where's Kabir?

457
00:28:28,291 --> 00:28:30,584
She was right there.

458
00:28:33,880 --> 00:28:35,756
Sanji!

459
00:28:41,221 --> 00:28:42,721
- No.
- Eva.

460
00:28:42,722 --> 00:28:44,139
Hold on.

461
00:28:44,140 --> 00:28:45,182
- No!
- No!

462
00:28:45,183 --> 00:28:48,185
No!

463
00:28:48,186 --> 00:28:49,603
Don't let go!

464
00:28:49,604 --> 00:28:51,188
Eva!

465
00:28:58,905 --> 00:29:00,406
Did you just see
what I just saw?

466
00:29:00,407 --> 00:29:02,533
Because my sense of reality
is really cocked up right now.

467
00:29:02,534 --> 00:29:03,784
Yeah, yeah, if you saw
that rock wall

468
00:29:03,785 --> 00:29:05,369
suck Eva into it,
then yeah, I saw it.

469
00:29:05,370 --> 00:29:06,996
Yeah, I did, but that's--

470
00:29:06,997 --> 00:29:08,664
that's totally
frickin' crazy, right?

471
00:29:08,665 --> 00:29:09,873
Look, there has to be
a seam or something

472
00:29:09,874 --> 00:29:11,918
that we can pry open.
Can you see anything?

473
00:29:16,923 --> 00:29:19,049
Mate, there's nothing.
I've got nothing.

474
00:29:19,050 --> 00:29:20,926
Damn it.

475
00:29:20,927 --> 00:29:22,511
I wonder why
we weren't taken.

476
00:29:22,512 --> 00:29:24,388
Look, the only difference
I can think is that we're men.

477
00:29:24,389 --> 00:29:25,931
Right, it's only the women
who've been taken.

478
00:29:25,932 --> 00:29:27,516
Right?
- Yeah, why would that be?

479
00:29:27,517 --> 00:29:29,060
I don't know, mate!

480
00:29:30,729 --> 00:29:32,938
All right, look,
maybe if they can hear us,

481
00:29:32,939 --> 00:29:34,481
they can--they can let us in
from the other side.

482
00:29:34,482 --> 00:29:36,692
Yeah? Yeah.
Sharon!

483
00:29:36,693 --> 00:29:39,445
Sharon, can you hear us?
- Eva!

484
00:29:39,446 --> 00:29:41,822
Kimi!
Dr. Kabir!

485
00:30:16,399 --> 00:30:20,527
Ian, shine your light here.

486
00:30:20,528 --> 00:30:22,654
Look.

487
00:30:22,655 --> 00:30:24,531
Something's moving
inside of these.

488
00:30:24,532 --> 00:30:26,450
Oh, don't--
don't touch it.

489
00:30:26,451 --> 00:30:31,121
Maybe if we follow it,
it might lead us somewhere.

490
00:30:37,253 --> 00:30:40,464
Oh, Brice, look at this.

491
00:30:40,465 --> 00:30:43,217
It looks different.

492
00:30:43,218 --> 00:30:46,136
Yeah, like it's been
touched a lot.

493
00:30:51,142 --> 00:30:52,601
Come on.
- Oh, no.

494
00:30:52,602 --> 00:30:53,852
What?

495
00:30:53,853 --> 00:30:54,937
It's gonna be
getting dark outside.

496
00:30:54,938 --> 00:30:57,439
We should have left already.
- Oh, God.

497
00:31:00,485 --> 00:31:04,405
Yes!

498
00:31:04,406 --> 00:31:05,865
Come on.

499
00:31:10,912 --> 00:31:12,746
- Eva! Eva!
- Sharon.

500
00:31:15,750 --> 00:31:19,294
Let go!

501
00:31:20,880 --> 00:31:22,840
Okay, I've got you.

502
00:31:22,841 --> 00:31:25,008
Come here.
Okay. Come on. okay.

503
00:31:28,096 --> 00:31:29,513
- Can you breathe?
- Yeah.

504
00:31:29,514 --> 00:31:31,306
You okay?
Sanji!

505
00:31:34,978 --> 00:31:38,689
Come on.

506
00:31:38,690 --> 00:31:42,776
Easy, all right.

507
00:31:42,777 --> 00:31:45,320
Hey, you're okay.

508
00:31:45,321 --> 00:31:47,323
Uh, guys.

509
00:32:01,254 --> 00:32:03,881
- Brice is alive?
- Long story.

510
00:32:20,982 --> 00:32:22,900
Let's get the hell
out of here.

511
00:32:31,785 --> 00:32:33,452
Do me a favor, everyone.

512
00:32:33,453 --> 00:32:35,329
Stay away from the walls.

513
00:32:35,330 --> 00:32:37,456
What did you guys see when
you went through the walls?

514
00:32:37,457 --> 00:32:39,083
It all happened so fast.

515
00:32:39,084 --> 00:32:40,417
As far as I could tell,

516
00:32:40,418 --> 00:32:42,002
the walls swallowed me
and spit me into

517
00:32:42,003 --> 00:32:44,129
that web thing in one motion.
- Same here.

518
00:32:44,130 --> 00:32:46,799
By the time I was in,
my arms were already pinned.

519
00:32:46,800 --> 00:32:48,133
I've never felt
more helpless.

520
00:32:48,134 --> 00:32:50,177
- Why is it doing this?
- I don't know.

521
00:32:50,178 --> 00:32:51,637
But I don't want
to give this place

522
00:32:51,638 --> 00:32:52,930
another chance to trap us.

523
00:32:52,931 --> 00:32:54,681
We need to move faster.

524
00:32:54,682 --> 00:32:56,558
We're barely gonna
beat the sun.

525
00:33:02,023 --> 00:33:04,483
Go! Go!
Go, go, go, go!

526
00:33:09,447 --> 00:33:11,115
Sharon, what about Doc?

527
00:33:11,116 --> 00:33:13,492
We'll check from above!

528
00:33:13,493 --> 00:33:15,245
Come on!
- Go, go, go!

529
00:33:25,005 --> 00:33:26,256
What the hell?

530
00:33:27,674 --> 00:33:29,341
No.

531
00:33:29,342 --> 00:33:31,385
Look, me and Doc,
we were just down there.

532
00:33:31,386 --> 00:33:33,220
You said you sent
the signal from there.

533
00:33:33,221 --> 00:33:34,847
Yeah, from the comms array.

534
00:33:34,848 --> 00:33:37,099
But it's the last thing
I remember doing.

535
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:39,893
James, we tracked that signal
to the underground room

536
00:33:39,894 --> 00:33:41,520
where we found you.

537
00:33:41,521 --> 00:33:43,856
The signal came
from room you were in.

538
00:33:43,857 --> 00:33:47,359
Your WD was wired
into the wall somehow.

539
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:49,195
Got a visual ahead.

540
00:33:52,741 --> 00:33:55,367
No.

541
00:33:55,368 --> 00:33:58,662
Can you get
close on her face?

542
00:34:03,043 --> 00:34:05,711
Turn it off.

543
00:34:05,712 --> 00:34:07,337
Sorry.

544
00:34:07,338 --> 00:34:13,927
There's no way she could
survive those injuries.

545
00:34:13,928 --> 00:34:16,472
So she couldn't have
been with you, James.

546
00:34:16,473 --> 00:34:18,515
I'm sorry.

547
00:34:23,146 --> 00:34:26,023
We received Marsh's
forensic report.

548
00:34:26,024 --> 00:34:28,525
The blood on the sword
was Hammond's,

549
00:34:28,526 --> 00:34:30,527
but the only fingerprints
found were yours.

550
00:34:30,528 --> 00:34:32,863
Well,
of course my fingerprints

551
00:34:32,864 --> 00:34:34,824
were on it.
It's my sword.

552
00:34:39,954 --> 00:34:41,246
I'm sorry.

553
00:34:41,247 --> 00:34:43,916
I'm not used to being on
this side of the situation.

554
00:34:43,917 --> 00:34:45,209
Because you're
not a murderer.

555
00:34:45,210 --> 00:34:47,336
Ms. Fowler, please.

556
00:34:47,337 --> 00:34:49,254
You don't know him.

557
00:34:49,255 --> 00:34:51,131
Felix is not a murderer.

558
00:34:51,132 --> 00:34:53,467
Ms. Fowler,
we allowed you to come with us

559
00:34:53,468 --> 00:34:55,929
under the agreement
that you do as we say.

560
00:35:02,894 --> 00:35:05,729
I'm only going to ask you
this once, Felix.

561
00:35:05,730 --> 00:35:08,107
Did you do this?

562
00:35:08,108 --> 00:35:09,692
Of course not.

563
00:35:09,693 --> 00:35:11,360
I barely knew Admiral Hammond,

564
00:35:11,361 --> 00:35:13,113
let alone had a reason
to kill him.

565
00:35:16,366 --> 00:35:17,909
I'll get to the bottom
of this.

566
00:35:20,120 --> 00:35:21,705
What about my daughter?

567
00:35:23,873 --> 00:35:26,542
That's why I came,
to offer to take care of her

568
00:35:26,543 --> 00:35:28,127
for as long as you need.

569
00:35:28,128 --> 00:35:29,962
Thank you.

570
00:35:29,963 --> 00:35:31,714
Are you sure she's who you
want watching over your child?

571
00:35:31,715 --> 00:35:34,925
We can make other arrangements.

572
00:35:34,926 --> 00:35:38,721
Whoever's setting me up
could come for Katheryn.

573
00:35:38,722 --> 00:35:42,099
Kelly can protect her.

574
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:44,436
As you wish.

575
00:35:46,313 --> 00:35:47,480
Zed...

576
00:35:50,108 --> 00:35:52,317
I am innocent.

577
00:35:52,318 --> 00:35:55,321
I believe you.

578
00:35:58,033 --> 00:36:01,910
I'll bring Katheryn
to visit every day.

579
00:36:27,812 --> 00:36:28,812
Well?

580
00:36:28,813 --> 00:36:31,482
What did those
web things do to us?

581
00:36:31,483 --> 00:36:33,817
Nothing to the three
of you or me,

582
00:36:33,818 --> 00:36:35,986
at least not that I can see.

583
00:36:35,987 --> 00:36:38,572
Just a tiny scar from
that hole behind our ears

584
00:36:38,573 --> 00:36:40,616
where that tube was attached
to all of us.

585
00:36:40,617 --> 00:36:42,701
And the tube
didn't do anything?

586
00:36:42,702 --> 00:36:45,621
Not to us,
at least not that I can find.

587
00:36:45,622 --> 00:36:47,206
What about Brice?

588
00:36:47,207 --> 00:36:49,375
Yeah, what about Brice?

589
00:36:49,376 --> 00:36:52,795
You, sir, are like
some kind of frickin' miracle.

590
00:36:52,796 --> 00:36:57,424
There are no signs of trauma,
no scars, nothing.

591
00:36:57,425 --> 00:36:58,884
You're healthy as a horse.

592
00:36:58,885 --> 00:37:00,594
But you saw
the life sign data

593
00:37:00,595 --> 00:37:02,554
when we left Ross
the first time.

594
00:37:02,555 --> 00:37:04,515
He was dead.
- I know.

595
00:37:04,516 --> 00:37:06,183
I saw the recording
of the attack too.

596
00:37:06,184 --> 00:37:09,561
He should, at the very least,
have scars all over his body.

597
00:37:09,562 --> 00:37:11,313
Is there any chance
he's one of us?

598
00:37:11,314 --> 00:37:12,982
A clone?
No.

599
00:37:12,983 --> 00:37:14,358
I checked his DNA.

600
00:37:14,359 --> 00:37:16,068
He's James Brice Prime.

601
00:37:16,069 --> 00:37:17,194
Guys, I am telling you,

602
00:37:17,195 --> 00:37:18,779
it seemed like
Doc was there, right?

603
00:37:18,780 --> 00:37:20,906
And she was the one
who fixed me up.

604
00:37:20,907 --> 00:37:23,993
James, you saw her body,
or what was left of it.

605
00:37:23,994 --> 00:37:25,703
Even if she was alive
and she had

606
00:37:25,704 --> 00:37:27,830
the best surgery facility...
- No.

607
00:37:27,831 --> 00:37:29,832
Ever built
and a staff of experts,

608
00:37:29,833 --> 00:37:32,084
she couldn't have healed you
to this extent.

609
00:37:32,085 --> 00:37:33,252
Okay, okay.

610
00:37:33,253 --> 00:37:35,254
But then what about
the Raiders, right?

611
00:37:35,255 --> 00:37:36,588
Were they at least real?

612
00:37:36,589 --> 00:37:39,967
I'm afraid it was
all in your head, James.

613
00:37:39,968 --> 00:37:42,261
But it's possible
that thing going in

614
00:37:42,262 --> 00:37:44,763
behind your ear
induced vivid dreams.

615
00:37:44,764 --> 00:37:46,849
No, no.

616
00:37:46,850 --> 00:37:49,560
Look, it was too real.
- I get it, believe me.

617
00:37:49,561 --> 00:37:51,895
Dreams can feel
pretty bloody real.

618
00:37:51,896 --> 00:37:54,064
Okay, so you think
that the webbing

619
00:37:54,065 --> 00:37:55,858
that was attached to him
healed him?

620
00:37:55,859 --> 00:37:57,943
I don't know what else
it could be.

621
00:37:57,944 --> 00:37:59,987
But that doesn't explain
why we were caught in it.

622
00:37:59,988 --> 00:38:02,573
- Yeah, or why I wasn't.
- We didn't need healing.

623
00:38:02,574 --> 00:38:04,283
And we didn't dream
or hallucinate

624
00:38:04,284 --> 00:38:05,784
when we were in them.

625
00:38:05,785 --> 00:38:08,370
Maybe because we never
lost consciousness.

626
00:38:08,371 --> 00:38:10,289
Well, I--

627
00:38:10,290 --> 00:38:14,793
I won't pretend
I understand any of it,

628
00:38:14,794 --> 00:38:19,923
but I'm really glad
you're back among the living.

629
00:38:19,924 --> 00:38:23,762
Did you miss me, Sharon?

630
00:38:27,265 --> 00:38:28,724
Mm.

631
00:38:28,725 --> 00:38:32,561
Come on.

632
00:38:34,606 --> 00:38:37,733
Oh, there is
one more weird thing.

633
00:38:37,734 --> 00:38:39,943
When Lieutenant Brice
left "Ark 1,"

634
00:38:39,944 --> 00:38:41,904
he didn't have an appendix.

635
00:38:41,905 --> 00:38:43,447
Yeah, I got it taken out
in college.

636
00:38:43,448 --> 00:38:45,491
I know.
I saw it on your charts.

637
00:38:45,492 --> 00:38:46,992
But guess what?

638
00:38:46,993 --> 00:38:51,872
Now you have a healthy,
intact appendix.

639
00:38:51,873 --> 00:38:54,625
- I'm sorry, what?
- What?

640
00:39:05,679 --> 00:39:07,429
- It's been way too long.
- Yeah.

641
00:39:07,430 --> 00:39:08,847
Yeah.

642
00:39:08,848 --> 00:39:11,101
We've got a lot
of catching up to do.

643
00:39:17,148 --> 00:39:19,108
Really?
Here?

644
00:39:19,109 --> 00:39:20,359
Right here?

645
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:24,363
Well, the next shift
won't come in for an hour,

646
00:39:24,364 --> 00:39:27,825
unless you want to wait.

647
00:39:27,826 --> 00:39:29,493
No.
Hell, no.

648
00:39:29,494 --> 00:39:32,204
No, no, no, no, no.

649
00:39:52,851 --> 00:39:54,977
Just ignore it.

650
00:39:54,978 --> 00:39:58,231
- It's the genomic sequencer.
- It can wait.

651
00:40:08,992 --> 00:40:12,411
Are you serious right now?
- I'm sorry.

652
00:40:12,412 --> 00:40:14,121
It's the results of
our first few samples.

653
00:40:14,122 --> 00:40:16,123
You're not
the least bit curious?

654
00:40:16,124 --> 00:40:19,877
Yeah, but at
this very moment--

655
00:40:21,171 --> 00:40:23,005
Okay. I'm just--

656
00:40:23,006 --> 00:40:25,759
I'm just gonna mute it.
I'm just gonna mute it, okay?

657
00:40:30,972 --> 00:40:33,932
Oh! Oh, man!
- What?

658
00:40:33,933 --> 00:40:36,894
The sequencer analyzed
the grass sample--

659
00:40:36,895 --> 00:40:39,480
<i>Cynodon dactylon.</i>

660
00:40:39,481 --> 00:40:41,732
It's Bermuda grass.

661
00:40:41,733 --> 00:40:44,777
You mean it's
like Bermuda grass.

662
00:40:44,778 --> 00:40:48,238
No, it's--
it's Bermuda grass.

663
00:40:48,239 --> 00:40:51,785
It was all over Earth.
- Well, what about the bird?

664
00:40:54,663 --> 00:40:57,873
<i>Turdus merula,</i>
Eurasian blackbird.

665
00:40:57,874 --> 00:41:01,669
And the tree?
Common black poplar.

666
00:41:03,296 --> 00:41:06,423
How is that possible?

667
00:41:12,555 --> 00:41:15,099
Come to bed, James.

668
00:41:15,100 --> 00:41:17,685
I'm still waiting for you
to fix me...

669
00:41:20,355 --> 00:41:21,898
Buddy.

670
00:41:25,318 --> 00:41:27,736
- James?
- Huh?

671
00:41:27,737 --> 00:41:30,864
Are you sure you're okay?

672
00:41:30,865 --> 00:41:33,033
Um...

673
00:41:33,034 --> 00:41:37,621
Yeah, no, I'm fine.
Yeah.

674
00:41:37,622 --> 00:41:39,999
Well, you still
haven't received

675
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:42,459
a proper homecoming yet.

